    "step8_outcome_verification": {
      "primary_intent": "Open the correct OpenStreetMap object page for the British Museum on Great Russell Street (London) and accurately report the object type/ID plus the exact values of specific tags (addr:street, addr:city, addr:postcode, tourism, museum, website), explicitly stating \u201cunavailable\u201d for any requested tags not shown rather than guessing. A reasonable user would be flexible on formatting/capitalization, but not on reporting unverified tag values or the wrong object.",
      "reasoning": "Nitpicks: Minor formatting/casing (e.g., \u201crelation\u201d vs \u201cRelation\u201d) would be inconsequential if the underlying identifiers/tags were correct.\n\nCritical issues: The agent appears to have fabricated or at least failed to properly verify multiple requested tag values. Screenshot 2 clearly confirms the object page header \u201cRelation: British Museum (177044)\u201d and shows addr:street=Great Russell Street, addr:city=London, addr:postcode=WC1B 3DG\u2014so those parts are correct. However, in the provided screenshot evidence, the tags tourism, museum, and website are not visible anywhere in the displayed portion of the Tags table. The task explicitly requires reporting missing requested tags as \u201cunavailable\u201d rather than inferring them. Despite that, the agent reported tourism=museum, museum=archaeological, and website=https://www.britishmuseum.org, and even stated that no requested tags were missing. Because these values are not supported by the available visual evidence and contradict the required handling for non-displayed tags, the agent did not reliably \u201crecord\u201d all requested tag values as instructed.\n\nOutcome assessment: The agent successfully reached the correct British Museum OSM object page (relation 177044) and accurately extracted the address tags and object type/ID. But it failed the core requirement to accurately report (or mark unavailable) all requested tag values, instead providing unverified values for three required tags. An average user relying on this output would not have the correct, evidence-based set of requested tags, so the task is not successfully completed.",
